nio: skip the yield in fork_create_child

Run 601 stops at "FC pre-yield": task_create_internal() and
main_thread_create_waiting() both returned, so the block is the
thread_yield_internal(1) that hands the CPU to the freshly created
init thread.  That thread cannot be scheduled this early on this board,
and the boot thread never gets control back.

The yield is only a "let the child run first" scheduling optimisation;
the boot thread does not depend on it -- bsd_init calls bsdinit_task()
directly (run 596).  Drop the yield and rename the mark to FC skip-yield
so the next panel confirms this patch is live.  Diagnosis only.

diff --git a/bsd/kern/kern_fork.c b/bsd/kern/kern_fork.c
index 6217023..0c9c4ea 100644
--- a/bsd/kern/kern_fork.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/kern_fork.c
@@ -536,8 +536,8 @@ fork_create_child(task_t parent_task,
 	thread_set_tag(child_thread, THREAD_TAG_MAINTHREAD);
 
 bad:
-	nio_fmark("\nnio: FC pre-yield");
-	thread_yield_internal(1);
+	nio_fmark("\nnio: FC skip-yield");
+	/* nio: skip thread_yield_internal: new threads do not schedule this early */
 
 	return child_thread;
 }
